/*

The inputs to this function are:
- m: number of rows of the source matrix (in complex elements).
- n: number of columns of the source matrix (in complex elements).
- a: pointer to the source m-by-n complex matrix A; each complex element is stored as two consecutive doubles: real then imaginary.
- lda: leading dimension of A, in complex elements (the number of complex elements between the start of consecutive rows in memory).
- offset: diagonal offset used to decide which elements are on/above/below the diagonal.
- b: pointer to an output buffer of complex elements (stored as interleaved real/imag doubles) that this function writes in a packed, block-by-block column format.

Behavior (scalar semantics):
- Let K be the number of columns processed per block (not explicitly provided by the interface). Columns are handled in blocks of width w, where w = min(K, n - col0) for each block starting at column col0 = 0, K, 2K, ...
- For each block starting at column col0 with width w, the function conceptually performs:
  for (long i = 0; i < m; i++) {
    for (long j = 0; j < w; j++) {
      long col = col0 + j;
      // Output location for this block is arranged as m consecutive row-slices,
      // each row-slice containing w complex elements.
      // The complex element b_out corresponds to b[2*(block_base + i*w + j)] (real) and +1 (imag),
      // where block_base is the total number of complex elements stored by all previous blocks.
      if (col == i - offset) {
        b_out.real = 1.0;
        b_out.imag = 0.0;
      } else if (col > i - offset) {
        b_out.real = a[2*(i*lda + col) + 0];
        b_out.imag = a[2*(i*lda + col) + 1];
      } else {
        // Below the diagonal: this output complex element is not modified.
      }
    }
